People sometimes ask me what actually happens when a doula stays the night. The honest answer is that most of it is quiet.

The house is dark, the parents are asleep, and my job is to keep it that way for as long as the baby allows. The bag I bring for that is small. Nothing in it is special on its own, but every item is there for a reason, and the reasons say more about overnight care than the items do.

## The things that keep the night dark

**A small, warm light.** An overhead light wakes everyone up, including the baby. I bring a soft light I can keep low, enough to see a diaper, a bottle or a latch, and not much more. Newborns are still learning that night is different from day, and a dim room is a kind way to help with that.

**Soft socks, or quiet shoes for inside.** I will walk the same hallway many times in one night. Floors creak, doors click, and a sleeping mother hears everything. I want to be a presence you do not hear.

**My phone, on silent.** It works as a clock and a timer, and that is all. The screen stays dim and the sound stays off.

None of this is about being invisible for its own sake. It is about protecting the one thing you cannot get back in the first weeks, which is a stretch of real sleep.

## The notebook that lets you sleep

The most important thing in my bag is a paper notebook and a pen.

Through the night, I write down what happened. When the baby ate and how much, the diapers, how long each stretch of sleep lasted, what helped them settle and what did not. If I notice something worth mentioning to your pediatrician or lactation consultant, it goes in the notebook too, so it is not lost by morning.

Why paper? Because in the morning you should not have to rebuild the night from memory. The notebook means you can sleep fully, instead of half-listening from the next room, because nothing is going to slip through.

If you are breastfeeding, I bring the baby to you when it is time to feed. Then I take care of everything around it, the burping, the diaper, the settling back down, so you can close your eyes again as soon as possible.

If the baby is taking bottles, of breast milk or formula, the night can look different, depending on your feeding plan. Both are good choices, and the notebook works the same way for either.

When you wake up, the night is written down. You can read it with your coffee, or you can not read it at all. It is there for you, not for a grade.

## The things that are for me, so they are not for you

**Water and my own food.** I bring what I need to eat and drink through the night. A family with a newborn should not be thinking about feeding a guest.

**A warm layer.** Houses get cold at 3 a.m., and many families keep the temperature where the baby is comfortable. A sweater in my bag means I never have to ask.

**Hand cream.** I wash my hands more times in one night than I can count. It is a small thing, but hands that hold a newborn should be gentle hands.

The idea behind all of these is simple. When I walk in the door, I do not want to add anything to your list. You should not have to show me where things are more than once, lend me anything, or wonder whether I am comfortable.

## A short list of people to call

I also keep a short list of local resources, the kind of people and places a family may need in the first months. Most nights I never open it.

But I am not a medical provider, and some questions belong to someone else. When one comes up, I want to be able to point you to the right person in the morning, not leave you alone with a search bar. And if something about you or your baby ever seems off during the night, I wake you, and we reach out to your provider together.

## What does not fit in the bag

The things that matter most on an overnight shift are not things.

Patience at 3 a.m., when a baby has been fed, changed and rocked, and still wants to be awake. Having no opinion about how you feed.

No judgment about the dishes in the sink. Knowing when to talk in the morning and when to just hand you the notebook and let you be.

I think about the mother on the other side of the door a lot during those hours. She is the reason I tiptoe.

When you wake up, I hope the first thing you feel is that someone was there.

The baby was held. The night was written down. {% sublinhado %}And for a few hours, you got to be the one who was cared for.{% /sublinhado %}
